    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d192-a5">

                <pb facs="0444" n="374" xml:id="pg374"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 5 in No. 135.—Extract.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Gaedeke to Mr. Sargent.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Consular Agency</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Königsberg</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1883-04-09"><hi rendition="#italic">April</hi> 9,
                        1883</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In pursuance of my communication of the
                    27th past, I have the honor to inform yon that I have ascertained that fourteen
                    cases of trichinosis have occurred at the garrison at Tilsit in the beginning of
                    March, but that all these cases have been very light, and that everybody has
                    already recovered.</p>
                <p>It is officially stated that the soldiers have not received the infected pork in
                    the <hi rendition="#italic">ménage</hi> of the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >caserne</hi>, but it has not been possible to state whence they got it, and
                    it is, therefore, impossible to say whether this pork has been of inland or of
                    American production, but it is most probable that the soldiers have received the
                    product from their relatives. * * *</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>CONRAD GAEDEKE.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
